## Values Live Until End of Scope

A scope is a block `{ }`. Values die at the closing brace:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let x = 5;
  {
    let y = &x;   // y borrows x
    io.println(y); // OK — y is alive here
  }  // y dies here — borrow ends
  // x is still alive — y's borrow ended before x
  return x;
}
```

### Borrows Must Die Before Owners

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let outer = Vec[Int].new();
  {
    let inner = Vec[Int].new();
    // let r = &inner;  // OK — r dies before inner
  }
  // inner is dead — can't reference it anymore
  return outer.len() as Int;
}
```

### Non-Lexical Lifetimes

XIOM is smart about borrow lifetimes. A borrow ends at its LAST USE, not at the scope end:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(1);
  let r = &data;
  io.println(r.len());  // Last use of r
  // r is dead here — even though the scope continues
  let w = &mut data;    // OK! r is already dead
  w.push(2);
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

This is called NLL (Non-Lexical Lifetimes). It makes writing safe code much easier.

